Undocumented and non-trivial: It hides a lookup that only works for some
(mode, kind) pairs, an inverted kernel-versus-other renderer choice, and an
output rule (--cmp-out) that exists to preserve file timestamps for a build
script. Suggested docstring:

    """Render one generated file of a family and write it out.

    Called by main() for --mode with --header or --source. The kernel mode
    calls gen_kernel.render_header() or render_source(); --schema-only leaves the
    family API out of the kernel output (family_api=False). Every other mode is
    looked up in the GENERATORS table by (mode, kind), where kind is "header" or
    "source"; at present only ('uapi', 'header') is there.

    With no -o/--output the text goes to standard output. Otherwise it is written
    to that file. With --cmp-out, an existing file whose contents are already
    identical is not rewritten, so its modification time stays put. The write is
    not atomic and does not create parent directories, unlike
    outputs.reconcile(), which is what the manifest actions use.

    Args:
      args: argparse.Namespace from main(), already validated. Uses mode, header,
        schema_only, output and cmp_out. Not modified.
      family: the resolved family returned by semantics.load().

    Returns:
      None: the result is the side effect of writing the text.

    Throws:
      KeyError: for --mode uapi with --source, since GENERATORS has no such entry
        and main() does not reject the combination. It is not a CtlvError, so it
        is not caught there.
      CtlvError: when a generator rejects the family. main() reports it.
      OSError: when the output file cannot be read or written.
    """

