Undocumented and non-trivial: It computes the worst-case payload length of a
node by wire kind, with sizing rules (NUL terminator, bool as one byte, blob
as descriptor attrs) that depend on conventions elsewhere, and it is mutually
recursive with attr_max_len through _children_max_len. Suggested docstring:

    """Return the maximum payload length of node, without its attr header.

    The payload is what follows the ATTR_HDR_LEN header of one occurrence of an
    attr, or of the message root. The rule depends on the declared kind:

      - uint, sint, enum, flags: SCALAR_PAYLOAD_LEN (8); all are 64-bit on the
        wire.
      - bool: BOOL_PAYLOAD_LEN (1); the padding to eight bytes is a placement
        matter and is added by the caller, not here.
      - string: max-length plus one for the terminating NUL, which max-length
        excludes.
      - binary: max-length.
      - blob: the widest backing among the declared ones, counted as that many
        core scalar descriptor attrs (BLOB_DESCRIPTOR_ATTRS), each aligned to
        ALIGNTO. The blob's own content is not part of the message.
      - anything else (nested), and the root: the sum over the children, see
        _children_max_len(), which multiplies each child by its
        max-occurrences and pads it.

    The result is a worst case used by attr_max_len(), root_max_len() and the
    inline message limit checks in compute() and composed(). It is only defined
    for trees that are bounded; see Throws.

    Args:
      node: semantics.Node of the expanded op tree. A root has no attr, other
        nodes have attr.decl (a spec.AttrDecl) giving the kind and limits.

    Returns:
      int: the payload length in bytes, not aligned.

    Throws:
      AssertionError: when a nested child has unlimited occurrences or re-enters
        an ancestor (a recursive attr set), since such a tree has no maximum.
        Semantic checks are supposed to reject these before sizes is used, so
        this signals a bug in the caller rather than a user error.
      KeyError: when a blob backing is missing from BLOB_DESCRIPTOR_ATTRS.
    """

